)}))\n  1377\t\n  1378\t\n  1379\t(def stdlib-forms\n  1380\t  \"The standard forms as a row-native batch: one tree defining `defn`,\n  1381\t   declared and harvested. Seed a store by expanding it (`seed-store`).\"\n  1382\t  (let [def-ast {:type :application,\n  1383\t                 :operator {:type :variable, :name 'yin/def},\n  1384\t                 :operands [{:type :literal, :value 'defn} defn-lambda-ast]}]\n  1385\t    [:yin.program/batch\n  1386\t     [(ast->packet def-ast)]\n  1387\t     0\n  1388\t     [[:yin.macro/definition 0 []]]\n  1389\t     [[:yin.macro/harvest 0 0 []]]]))\n  1390\t\n  1391\t\n  1392\t(defn seed-store\n  1393\t  \"Fold validated input batches in order through `expand-batch`, returning\n  1394\t   the resulting store. Throws on a batch that fails.\"\n  1395\t  [ctx batches]\n  1396\t  (:store (reduce (fn [ctx batch]\n  1397\t                    (let [{:keys [status error] :as r} (expand-batch batch ctx)]\n  1398\t                      (when-not (= :ok status)\n  1399\t                        (throw (ex-info \"Store seeding batch failed\" error)))\n  1400\t                      (:ctx r)))\n  1401\t                  ctx\n  1402\t                  batches)))\n  1403\t\n  1404\t\n  1405\t;; =============================================================================\n  1406\t;; Expander observer (§9)\n  1407\t;; =============================================================================\n  1408\t\n  1409\t(defn make-expander\n  1410\t  \"The observer consumer: `ctx`, the `program-out` writer, and an optional\n  1411\t   log writer, with nothing staged.\"\n  1412\t  ([ctx out] (make-expander ctx out nil))\n  1413\t  ([ctx out log]\n  1414\t   {:ctx ctx, :out out, :out-staged nil, :log log, :log-staged nil,\n  1415\t    :forwarded 0, :errors []}))\n  1416\t\n  1417\t\n  1418\t(defn ready?\n  1419\t  \"Ready only when both staged slots are empty.\"\n  1420\t  [expander]\n  1421\t  (and (nil? (:out-staged expander)) (nil? (:log-staged expander))))\n  1422\t\n  1423\t\n  1424\t(defn call-site-table\n  1425\t  \"The C1 side table the consumer `expander` holds for the occurrence\n  1426\t   the executed tree packet `tree` names — `origin` being the\n  1427\t   admission/expansion origin that occurrence was produced under (§2.1:\n  1428\t   an application site is K = [origin root-address path], so the site\n  1429\t   table's key is the origin beside the root address) — or nil when\n  1430\t   that occurrence made no C1 claim. Two expansions can produce the\n  1431\t   SAME final root from different admissions; keying by the full\n  1432\t   occurrence identity keeps their tables distinct, so resolving by the\n  1433\t   origin+root pair at hand never answers another execution's table,\n  1434\t   however many loads have run since, and an unclaimed load of a root\n  1435\t   previously loaded under a claim retrieves no stale table.\"\n  1436\t  [expander tree origin]\n  1437\t  (get (:call-site-tables expander) [origin (first tree)]))\n  1438\t\n  1439\t\n  1440\t(defn load\n  1441\t  \"Expand one observed batch once: keep the returned context, stage the\n  1442\t   tree only on success, stage the log when a log writer exists, and\n  1443\t   accumulate the error of a failed expansion. Expansion failure is\n  1444\t   data, so the cursor advances past it. A successful expansion's C1\n  1445\t   side table (the batch result's `:frontend-metadata`, present only\n  1446\t   when the selected member itself claimed coverage) is retained on the\n  1447\t   consumer under the occurrence identity of the staged tree — its\n  1448\t   expansion origin beside its final root address (`call-site-tables`,\n  1449\t   resolved by `call-site-table`) — so the composition that drives this\n  1450\t   expander — the REPL's session — holds every executed occurrence's\n  1451\t   coverage beside the expander that produced it, each load's table\n  1452\t   surviving the next, even when two loads land on the same root.\"\n 