{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"agent_message","text":"The overwrite and per-member claim defects are closed, but the origin semantics still conflict with the cited design: every final tree receives the input’s `:source` origin, including trees changed by macro expansion. The publisher also still derives its origin from the content root. I’m separating these remaining identity issues from the fixes that pass review."}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"agent_message","text":"**Request amendments before the codex gate/landing.** The collision and per-member fixes are sound, but the implementation still falls short of §2.1’s origin semantics.\n\n1. **P1 — Generated final trees retain the original source origin.** After expansion, tail marking, and stand-in lowering, the finalizer unconditionally uses `source-origin` ([macro.cljc:1307](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc:1307)). This distinguishes sequential admissions, but it does not identify generation. [Design §2.1:38](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/collab/yang-clojure/1794670000000-architect-callsite-generalization-design.gpt-6-astra.findings.md:38) explicitly permits the original source origin for a retained, no-op tree and prohibits labeling generated post-expansion trees as original source. The regression fixture actually asserts a changed final root while expecting the input source origin ([test:264](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/test/yang/clojure_callsite_test.cljc:264)).\n\n   **Amendment:** resolve the real generation provenance and final-root correspondence required by §2.3; retain source origins where applicable. Do not substitute an arbitrary intermediate expansion record or fabricate an expansion event. The new `[origin root]` indexing can remain.\n\n2. **P1 — Published origins still collapse admission into content identity.** `publication-origin` constructs `[:source :yin.vm.linker.publish/publish root 0]` ([publish.cljc:114](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc:114)). Consequently, separately published identical trees receive identical origins. Retrieval also reconstructs—and requires—that content-derived origin ([publish.cljc:377](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc:377)). This is a residual predecessor issue, not introduced by the new table key, but it contradicts §2.1’s distinct-admission requirement.\n\n   **Amendment:** supply an explicit publication admission identity, persist it in the side record, and retrieve that recorded identity. Add coverage distinguishing two admissions from replay of one recorded admission.\n\nThe remaining requested checks pass static review:\n\n- **Occurrence indexing:** `[origin root]` correctly partitions tables whose rows retain `path`; lookup uses precisely the same pair. Same-root admissions no longer overwrite each other, and an unclaimed later admission cannot retrieve the earlier table ([macro.cljc:1424](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc:1424), [macro.cljc:1460](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc:1460)).\n- **Member verdicts:** eager `mapv` validates every member before `some` examines completed verdicts. Finalization reads only `run-index`’s verdict. Both round-3 defects are closed ([encoder.cljc:333](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/src/cljc/yin/vm/encoder.cljc:333), [macro.cljc:1319](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc:1319)).\n- **Consumers and retrieval:** no stale two-argument callers found. The oracle independently constructs its existing site table ([oracle:2464](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:2464)). Manifest references now carry the table and record through closure transfer ([closure.cljc:195](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/closure.cljc:195)).\n- **Boundaries:** canonical code rows remain unchanged; provenance stays in side data. Schema remains `1`, with the optional reference accepted as authorized here. Five-element batches remain valid ([macro.cljc:381](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc:381)). Publisher version rejection correctly uses key presence, including `nil` and `false` ([publish.cljc:237](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc:237)).\n\nStatic review of `b825498e..3cd78223`; the named saved diff was absent. No files changed or tests run. The supplied Jupiter results support the repaired behaviors, but do not resolve these origin-contract findings."}}
